  <h1>India’s Urban Flooding — Causes, Consequences, and Mitigation</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Urban flooding has become a recurring crisis in India’s major cities like Bengaluru, Chennai, and Mumbai. In 2024, The Hindu reported widespread waterlogging after heavy monsoon showers, highlighting gaps in urban planning, drainage, and resilience. For UPSC, this issue reflects the convergence of climate change, governance, and urbanisation.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Chennai (2023): Floods displaced thousands, exposed drainage weaknesses.<br/>•	Bengaluru (2022–23): IT corridor submerged, economic losses in billions.<br/>•	Mumbai: Annual monsoon flooding continues despite investments.<br/>•	Urban Expansion: Wetlands and floodplains encroached in most metros.<br/>•	Climate Factor: IMD data shows increase in extreme rainfall events.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Causes<br/>1.	Unplanned Urbanisation: Encroachment on lakes, wetlands, floodplains.<br/>2.	Poor Drainage Infrastructure: Outdated systems, clogged by waste.<br/>3.	Climate Change: Intensified monsoon variability and cloudbursts.<br/>4.	Solid Waste Mismanagement: Plastic waste choking drains.<br/>5.	Institutional Fragmentation: Multiple agencies, weak accountability.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Consequences<br/>•	Human Impact: Loss of life, displacement, health risks (dengue, cholera).<br/>•	Economic Losses: IT sector losses in Bengaluru floods ~₹225 crore/day.<br/>•	Infrastructure Damage: Roads, housing, transport networks disrupted.<br/>•	Social Inequality: Slum dwellers most affected due to weak housing.<br/>•	Environmental Costs: Wetlands, aquifers, ecosystems degraded.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	Smart Cities Mission: Stormwater drainage upgrades.<br/>•	AMRUT Mission: Focus on water supply and drainage.<br/>•	National Disaster Management Authority (NDMA): Guidelines on urban flooding (2010).<br/>•	State Initiatives: Mumbai Stormwater Drainage Project (BRIMSTOWAD).<br/>•	Early Warning Systems: IMD, flood forecasting apps.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Awareness Growth: Media and civil society pressure for better planning.<br/>•	Technology Use: GIS mapping, flood modeling in some states.<br/>•	Integration Efforts: Some cities incorporating blue–green infrastructure.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Implementation Deficit: Projects delayed or poorly executed.<br/>•	Coordination Failures: Centre, state, and ULB overlap.<br/>•	Lack of Maintenance: Drains clogged due to weak municipal systems.<br/>•	Encroachments: Weak enforcement against illegal construction.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Urban Sustainability: Flood resilience key for smart cities.<br/>•	Economic Growth: Cities as engines of growth vulnerable to flooding.<br/>•	Climate Adaptation: Reflects India’s larger adaptation challenge.<br/>•	Social Justice: Poor disproportionately impacted.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-I (Geography): Climate change, disasters.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Urban local bodies, planning.<br/>•	GS-III (Environment): Climate adaptation, disaster management.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Urban flooding is not natural disaster but man-made failure.”<br/>o	“Resilient cities are the future of sustainable development.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Governance and environmental policy.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Restore Wetlands: Strict protection of urban lakes and floodplains.<br/>2.	Upgrade Drainage: Scientific stormwater systems, periodic desilting.<br/>3.	Blue–Green Infrastructure: Parks, permeable pavements, rainwater harvesting.<br/>4.	Strengthen ULBs: Funds and technical capacity for flood resilience.<br/>5.	Community Engagement: Citizen participation in waste management and monitoring.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Urban flooding is a policy failure compounded by climate stress. Building resilient, well-planned cities is critical for India’s urban future. For UPSC aspirants, this theme captures how climate, governance, and urbanisation intersect.</p>
